  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The plot given below shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> curves (where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is the pressure and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> is the temperature) for two solvents <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math> and isomolal solutions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi></math> in these solvents. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi></math> is completely dissociates in both the solvents.<br ><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/3ec0bbad-8543-43c0-a9b9-343c11a4d841-image.png\"/><br >On the addition of equal number of moles of a non-volatile solute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> in equal amount (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>kg</mi></math>) of these solvents, the elevation of boiling point of solvent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> is three times that of solvent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math>. The solute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> is known to undergo dimerization in these solvents. If the degree of dimerization is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn></math> in the solvent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math>, the degree of dimerization in the solvent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> is _______.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
